{"id": "section-body-24441", "title": "Kaposi’s sarcoma (KS)", "chapterId": "chapter-twenty-two", "tocLevel": 4, "tocPath": ["APPROACH TO PATIENTS WITH EMERGENCY CONDITIONS", "MALIGNANT DISEASE CONDITIONS", "Cancer of The Skin", "Kaposi’s sarcoma (KS)"], "blocks": [{"id": "body-24441", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Kaposi’s sarcoma (KS)", "runs": [{"text": "Kaposi’s"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "sarcoma"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "(KS)"}], "level": 4, "list": {"level": 2, "numberingId": "534"}}, {"id": "body-24442", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "It is a malignant tumour of angio–formative cells usually starting from the skin but occasionally involving many other body organs. Kaposi sarcoma can be primarily categorized into four types: epidemic of AIDS–related, immunocompromised, classic or sporadic, and endemic (African). Here we have non-AIDS related (endemic) KS and AIDS related (epidemic) KS, where the latter is more common, 80–85%.", "runs": [{"text": "It is a malignant tumour of angio–formative cells usually starting from the skin but occasionally involving many other body organs."}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Kaposi"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "sarcoma"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "can"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "be"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "primarily"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "categorized"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "into"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "four"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "types:"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "epidemic"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "of"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "AIDS–related,"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "immunocompromised,"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "classic or"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "sporadic,"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "and"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "endemic"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "(African)."}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Here"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "we"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "have"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "non-AIDS"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "related"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "(endemic)"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "KS"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "and"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "AIDS"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "related"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "(epidemic)"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "KS,"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "where"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "the latter is more common, 80–85%."}]}, {"id": "body-24444", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Clinical Presentation", "runs": [{"text": "Clinical"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Presentation"}], "level": 4}, {"id": "body-24445", "type": "paragraph", "style": "ListParagraph", "text": "KS presents as firm, dark brown nodules or plaques in the skin. Usually, more on the limbs", "runs": [{"text": "KS"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "presents"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "as"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "firm,"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "dark"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "brown"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "nodules"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "or"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "plaques"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "in"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "the"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "skin."}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Usually,"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "more"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "on"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "the"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "limbs"}], "list": {"level": 0, "numberingId": "446"}}, {"id": "body-24446", "type": "paragraph", "style": "ListParagraph", "text": "It presents as widespread lymphadenopathy with or without skin lesions in young children and those with immunodeficiency.", "runs": [{"text": "It"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "presents"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "as"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "widespread"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "lymphadenopathy"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "with"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "or"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "without"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "skin"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "lesions"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "in"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "young"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "children"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "and"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "those"}, {"text": " "}, {"text